Ohle Claussen!

> True, a Sudoku level would have quite a boring layout, gameplay-wise. It
> would be just plain Sudoku, without much NetNack interfering with it.

I agree. Sokoban has boulders that act like in all of Nethack, i.e. they
can be destroyed with pick-axes, force bolt, stone to flesh, /oStriking
etc., pits and holes, monsters can really ruin your day (like in all of
Nethack), and it generally fits in fine - you don't have to know Sokoban
to realize what you need to do to complete the Sokoban levels. Lots of
boulders and pits you need to cross are kind-of self-explaining.

> The point that randomization of Sudoku levels is easier is voided by the
> fact that writing a Sudoku solver that you just copy'n'paste your
> NetHack screen into is trivial.

True. Sudoku is very easily solved (in terms of algorithm complexity). Any
pre-school kid could write a solver and probably execute the steps
herself. It's kind of funny that you can apparently make money by selling
books full of Sudoku puzzles these days. Of course, solving Sokoban is
easy for a computer, too. It does generally require more backtracking,
though, which makes it more difficult for a human - because we can't
actually backtrack when we're playing.

> I'm afraid this is no improvement over Sokoban. As I see it, Sokoban is
> the perfect game to implement in NetHack. If it's too boring, either
> skip it or implement new levels. With some dozens of new levels to
> choose from, people might enjoy it again.

I personally never found Sokoban boring. I do believe that significantly
more Sokoban levels in the same game would be too tedious, but more levels
to choose from might be a good idea. Of course, people will use spoilers
anyway, if they can't solve them on their own, so any energy spent on
creating additional Sokoban levels is probably wasted. 

I think it was never meant to be a meaningful part of the game, anyway.
You don't have to complete it and it doesn't take much time.


